From 187a938e34bbf7833ebbf19c2bb5bf3d1e3ac7ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: csolomon <charliesolomon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 09:23:56 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] patch: restrict model sources (deny-all built-in catalog,
 hidden denied providers)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Re-authored against v0.84.4. The v0.79.1 version patched three sites in
model-registry.ts; upstream gutted that file from 37KB to a ~4.5KB
compatibility facade and moved the logic into model-runtime.ts and
provider-composer.ts, so none of the old anchors survived.

Two controls, both in ModelRuntime.create():

1. GAH_BUILTIN_MODELS — comma-separated provider/model-id globs. Unset or
   empty exposes NO built-in models.
2. GAH_ALLOW_MODELS_JSON must be "1" for models.json to be read at all. That
   file can define arbitrary provider ids with their own baseUrl/apiKey/
   headers, and its oauth:"radius" form injects directly into the builtins
   map, so it is an endpoint bypass rather than a preference.

Placement notes, each load-bearing:

* ModelRuntime.create() is the single constructor every entry point shares —
  main, the SDK, auth-check and package-manager-cli each build their own
  runtime. An extension-level control would cover TUI/RPC sessions only and
  leave `gah auth login <any-provider>` working.

* NOT provider-composer.ts. composeModelProvider() is skipped entirely when a
  provider has no models.json or extension overlay (the common case), and on
  any composition error recomposeProvider() falls back to the unfiltered
  built-in — it fails open.

* The allowlist wraps getModels() and is applied OUTERMOST, after
  withRemoteCatalog(). That overlay merges models fetched from the remote
  catalog on every call and did not exist in v0.79.1; filtering before it
  would let remotely-added models through.

* Gating the models.json PATH rather than ModelConfig.load() also neutralises
  the radius builtins injection and the re-read inside refresh().

Upstream's --models / enabledModels / /scoped-models are deliberately NOT used
for this: they scope one session's Ctrl+P set over an intact catalogue, the
full list stays reachable via /model and RPC, the setting is plain
user-writable settings.json with no managed tier, and selecting an
out-of-scope model auto-appends it to the list. Convenience, not a boundary.

The policy itself lives in a new file, src/core/gah-model-policy.ts — new
files never conflict on sync (patches/README.md).

Verified: with GAH_BUILTIN_MODELS unset, --list-models reports no models;
with 'anthropic/claude-opus-*' it lists only those.

Re-authored 2026-09-02 to fold in #18 -- the same control seen from the
provider-picker side. Kept in this patch rather than a 0012 because the
two share the import hunk in model-runtime.ts, and apply-patches.sh
cannot recognise overlapping patches as already applied.

Two gaps in the allowlist's UI surface (0010-restrict-model-sources), one
patch because they are the same control seen from two sides.

1. /login listed every built-in provider, including the ~38 whose models
   the allowlist denies (#18). 0010 wraps each provider's getModels() but
   never filters the provider array, so getLoginProviderOptions() -- and
   `gah auth`, and ModelRegistry.getProviders() for extensions -- offered
   providers that can never serve a model. A technician who logged in to
   one stored a credential on the shared host for nothing, and the deny-all
   posture was invisible at exactly the moment a provider was being chosen.

   ModelRuntime.getProviders() and getProvider() now hide providers whose
   catalogue is empty. The predicate is "has at least one model after
   filtering", read at call time; getModels() is synchronous everywhere
   (withRemoteCatalog merges an in-memory overlay that refreshModels()
   fills separately), so this is not a network round-trip per provider at
   menu render, which was the open question on #18. Internal paths keep
   reading this.models directly, so availability refresh and auth checks
   are unchanged. Hidden, not shown disabled: the approved set is what a
   user should be choosing from.

2. A native re-registration of a built-in escaped the allowlist. An
   extension can pi.registerProvider(providerObject); the policy pack does
   exactly that to strip OAuth flows when providers.json is present, taking
   the object from pi-ai's builtinProviders() -- upstream's raw catalogue,
   not the wrapped one create() built. recomposeProvider() then served it
   as-is. Reproduced at the runtime level: under deny-all, after that call,
   anthropic exposed 14 models. It stayed out of --list-models on this
   machine only because the stored credential was OAuth, which the
   re-registered provider no longer accepted; an API key would have shown
   them. Config-form overrides of a built-in id were already filtered
   (they compose over the wrapped base), so this makes native
   registration consistent with them.

   recomposeProvider() now routes every setProvider through a helper that
   re-wraps anything registered under a default built-in id. Idempotent
   over the already-wrapped builtins. Providers under new ids -- what
   providers.json normally registers -- are untouched and stay visible.

Why here and not the policy pack: same reasoning as 0010. The extension API
cannot see the runtime's providers at factory time (registrations are
queued until bindCore), and `gah auth`, the SDK and the package manager
build their own ModelRuntime before any extension loads. The provider list
and the recompose chokepoint are the two places every entry point shares.

Tests: test/gah-model-policy.test.ts (new file) covers deny-all, allowlist
matching, an allowlist entry that matches nothing, extension providers by
config and native under new ids, and the native re-registration of a
built-in. Four upstream tests now fail in addition to the seven that
already failed under GAH's deny-all default: two in
model-runtime-auth-options enumerate getProviders() expecting all
built-ins, and two in agent-session-dynamic-provider re-register
"anthropic" natively with a custom baseUrl -- the config-form twins of
those two were already failing for the same reason. All four assert the
behaviour this patch removes. This repo's CI does not run them.

Re-authored 2026-09-02 (#20): the policy header's note about the IAM
boundary now says "a governed deployment" rather than naming one
organisation. Comment only.

